The Racket binding gives you a Goblins-shaped `session` surface with native
values
([`pancake.rkt`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/bindings/racket/pancake.rkt)).
The transport modules are `pancake-tcp.rkt`, `pancake-inproc.rkt`, and lazy
`pancake-iroh.rkt`. The [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) page covers install,
and [modules]($link.page('modules')) has the actor vocabulary.

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip reads like this in Racket:

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:racket GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; Plain TCP uses a cleartext host and port.
> (define partner (tcp-locator "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define session (connect partner))
> (define greeter (session-fetch session "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send session greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! session)
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Partners dial the key, not the socket alone.
> (define partner (keyed-tcp-locator "<64 hex chars>" "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define session (connect partner))
> (define greeter (session-fetch session "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send session greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! session)
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; Same TCP sockets as keyed dialing; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream.
> (define partner (noise-tcp-locator "<64 hex chars>" "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define session (connect partner))
> (define greeter (session-fetch session "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send session greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! session)
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt"
>          "pancake-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> (define partner "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh")
>
> (define session (connect partner IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY))
> (define greeter (session-fetch session "greeter")) ; a capability, by name
> (printf "~a\n" (session-send session greeter 'greet "world"))
> (session-close! session)
> ```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:racket -->
```

The CapTP verbs stay the same across tabs. What changes is the locator helper
and whether CapTP rides cleartext or Noise. The iroh tab needs
`pancake-iroh.rkt` and a discovery-reachable node id.

## Hosting the other half

`(accept (listen …))` hands back the same `session` that `connect` returns.
Keyed and Noise listeners take a 32-byte seed so the advertised designator
survives a restart; iroh binds a node id instead of a host and port.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:listen:racket GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; Plain TCP binds the host and port you choose.
> (define server (listen "127.0.0.1" 9099))
> (define session (accept server))
> (session-publish session "greeter"
>                  (lambda (verb args)
>                    (format "hello, ~a" (car args))))
> (session-serve session)
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Persist the seed to keep the same key.
> (define seed (make-bytes 32 7)) ; Replace with durable secret bytes.
> (define server (listen-keyed-tcp "127.0.0.1" 9099 #:seed seed))
> (printf "~a\n" (keyed-tcp-server-uri server)) ; Hand this locator to partners.
> (define session (accept server))
> (session-publish session "greeter"
>                  (lambda (verb args)
>                    (format "hello, ~a" (car args))))
> (session-serve session)
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt")
>
> ;; Same bind as keyed-tcp; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> (define seed (make-bytes 32 7)) ; Replace with durable secret bytes.
> (define server (listen-noise-tcp "127.0.0.1" 9099 #:seed seed))
> (printf "~a\n" (noise-tcp-server-uri server)) ; Hand this locator to partners.
> (define session (accept server))
> (session-publish session "greeter"
>                  (lambda (verb args)
>                    (format "hello, ~a" (car args))))
> (session-serve session)
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "pancake.rkt"
>          "pancake-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; Bind a key, not a place. Partners can dial this node id from anywhere.
> (define server (listen-iroh (make-iroh-config IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY)))
> (printf "ocapn://~a.iroh\n" (iroh-server-node-id server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define session (iroh-server-accept server))
> (session-publish session "greeter"
>                  (lambda (verb args)
>                    (format "hello, ~a" (car args))))
> (session-serve session)
> ```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:listen:racket -->
```

## A Goblins-style actor API

The verbs are Goblins's; the substrate is pancake. This is not a Goblins
compatibility layer, and an existing Goblins program will not run on it
unchanged. You define an actor constructor with
`(define (^name bcom arg ...) ...)`, return its `methods` behavior, and then
`spawn` it. You call it near with `$`, send it eventually with `<-` or `<-np`,
observe a promise with `on`, and become a new behavior with `bcom`, all inside
`with-vat`. On top of that, `connect`, `session-fetch`, and `session-send` reach
a partner.

> **`$` and `<-`**, near-call and eventual-send, come straight from the
> [E language](http://erights.org/), and [The modules]($link.page('modules'))
> page defines both. Across a session only `<-` works, since a partner's object
> is never in your vat.

## Async

Concurrency is Racket's own green threads and ports. `(session-serve sess)`
pumps the inbound loop, so run it on its own green thread, and `(on vow ...)`
runs a callback when the vow (Goblins's word for a promise) settles.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the CapTP ideas.

The full surface lives in the
[Racket binding README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/bindings/racket/README.md).
Among the four bindings, only Racket keeps Goblins's verbs, so the
[rooms chat client](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/apps/rooms/clients/racket/room-client.rkt)
reads almost like Goblins code.
